DESIGN NOTES
================================================================================
* REUSE, not reinvention: the e2e test drives the SAME built CPU-ref executables + file-based .npy bridge as
  tests/kernels/conftest.py (imported as a module: SEED, exe paths, _require_exe, _drive_activation reused; no
  duplicated seed/paths/bridge) and asserts the SAME golden harness (tests/golden) thresholds (contract C via
  metrics' pinned constants). The traffic report reads the discardable attrs the fusion passes already attach.
* NO new polykernel ops, NO new declared op attributes, NO changes to the dialect/passes/kernels/golden harness.
  This task only READS existing discardable attrs and adds external Python tooling + reports.
* SCALE: the real mlp_block.mlir matmuls are ~92 GFLOP under the naive CPU ref (too large to execute in a test),
  so correctness is validated on a tractable mini-MLP (seq=32, d_model=64, ffn=128) with the IDENTICAL op
  structure; at this scale every op (incl. the two-reduction fused_rmsnorm_matmul) is BIT-EXACT vs the golden,
  so uniform contract C thresholds apply with no relaxed ceiling. The analytic traffic report covers the real
  shapes (shape arithmetic, no execution). A dedicated test confirms the real file fuses as expected (parsed).
* ADD op: kernels/cpu ships no add kernel (trivial elementwise op fully determined by the rounding contract);
  the harness computes it inline (fp32-add + bf16 RNE, identical to golden.add by construction) and flags it
  ref-only. The compute ops (rmsnorm, matmul, gelu, fused_rmsnorm_matmul) are validated against real executables.
